WEBVTT

00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:04.146
The unit circle extends trigonometry beyond acute triangle angles

00:00:04.146 --> 00:00:09.557
A point reached by turning through an angle has coordinates cosine and sine

00:00:09.557 --> 00:00:15.800
Because its distance from the origin is one, the coordinate squares always add to one.

00:00:17.100 --> 00:00:19.250
Turn into the second quadrant

00:00:19.250 --> 00:00:29.568
At one hundred twenty degrees, the horizontal coordinate is negative one half and the vertical coordinate is positive square root of three over two

00:00:29.568 --> 00:00:33.867
Quadrant signs come from actual coordinate directions, not memorized exceptions.

00:00:35.167 --> 00:00:43.450
Continuing to two hundred forty degrees keeps the horizontal coordinate negative but moves the point below the horizontal axis

00:00:43.450 --> 00:00:46.066
Sine is now negative as well

00:00:46.066 --> 00:00:51.733
A related reference triangle gives the magnitudes, while the quadrant supplies each sign.

00:00:53.033 --> 00:00:56.818
One full additional turn returns to the same point

00:00:56.818 --> 00:01:03.967
Sine and cosine therefore repeat after two pi radians, and many angles can share the same coordinates

00:01:03.967 --> 00:01:09.433
Angle size records the turning history, while the point records its final position.
